{
  "analysis": "Round 2 left two near-tied recipes: default inclusion 30/10/10 (mAP50 0.6334, patches 0.780, inclusion 0.457) vs small inclusion 0.3-0.7 30/10/10 (0.6303, inclusion 0.493 but patches -0.085). Inclusion is still the weakest class (112 missed, low-tertile recall 0.288, median missed 33px vs 90.5px train). prefer_small sources and any_class backgrounds were proven losses, so both are avoided. Final-round design: (A) repeat the default-scale recipe that held the mAP50 lead; (B) push small inclusion from 30 to 40 (headroom 45) to test the marginal value of more small inclusions vs starving patches/scratches to 5; (C) under small inclusion, add small patches 0.4-0.8 to recover the patches dip (missed patches median 54.6px, low-tertile recall 0.578; Round 1 small patches were not harmful to patches). The lab control repeats the kept small-inclusion 30/10/10 plan, so A/B/C each differ from it in one factor.",
  "candidates": [
    {
      "hypothesis": "Default-scale inclusion repeat: the Round-2 control recipe (default 0.6-1.4 inclusion, 30/10/10) had the highest mAP50 (0.6334) and best patches (0.780); the small-inclusion gain in inclusion AP50 (+0.036) was offset by the patches cost (-0.085). Repeating the default recipe in round 3 on the cumulative set should hold or extend the mAP50 lead. Confirmed if mAP50 >= 0.6334 with patches AP50 >= 0.75 and inclusion AP50 >= 0.45.",
      "allocation": {"inclusion": 30, "patches": 10, "scratches": 10},
      "params": {
        "inclusion": {"scale_min": 0.6, "scale_max": 1.4, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "uniform", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []},
        "patches": {"scale_min": 0.6, "scale_max": 1.4, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "uniform", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []},
        "scratches": {"scale_min": 0.6, "scale_max": 1.4, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "uniform", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []}
      }
    },
    {
      "hypothesis": "Inclusion-cap push: inclusion is still the worst class (AP50 0.493, low-tertile recall 0.288, 112 missed at ~33px) and small-scale 0.3-0.7 pastes are the proven lever for it; inclusion AP50 rose with every +30 small inclusions (0.329 -> 0.457 -> 0.493), so spending more of the 45 headroom (40 vs 30) should lift inclusion further, and the small reduction of patches/scratches to 5 each is tolerable since both sit near 0.70. Confirmed if inclusion AP50 > 0.50 and mAP50 beats the small-inclusion 30/10/10 control; if it loses, 30 is the sweet spot and allocation balance matters more than I thought.",
      "allocation": {"inclusion": 40, "patches": 5, "scratches": 5},
      "params": {
        "inclusion": {"scale_min": 0.3, "scale_max": 0.7, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "uniform", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []},
        "patches": {"scale_min": 0.6, "scale_max": 1.4, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "uniform", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []},
        "scratches": {"scale_min": 0.6, "scale_max": 1.4, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "uniform", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []}
      }
    },
    {
      "hypothesis": "Small-patches recovery: under small inclusion, patches AP50 dropped to 0.695 (vs 0.780 with default inclusion) because small inclusion pastes resemble small patches; missed patches are small (median 54.6px, low-tertile recall 0.578, n=90) and Round 1 showed 0.4-0.8x patches were not harmful to patches (0.785 vs 0.765 control). Adding small patches should sharpen the patch/inclusion boundary and recover the dip while keeping the inclusion gain. Confirmed if patches AP50 > 0.74, inclusion AP50 > 0.48, and mAP50 > 0.6334; if patches stay < 0.72, small patches do not fix the interaction and the default recipe (A) is the safer final bet.",
      "allocation": {"inclusion": 30, "patches": 10, "scratches": 10},
      "params": {
        "inclusion": {"scale_min": 0.3, "scale_max": 0.7, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "uniform", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []},
        "patches": {"scale_min": 0.4, "scale_max": 0.8, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "uniform", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []},
        "scratches": {"scale_min": 0.6, "scale_max": 1.4, "target_pool": "same_class", "source_weighting": "uniform", "max_overlap": 0.15, "sources": []}
      }
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Candidate 1 (default-scale inclusion 30/10/10) decisively wins: mAP50 0.6614, +0.0311 over the prior model and +0.0367 over the next-best (candidate 3, 0.6247) — well beyond the ~0.03 noise band. It also posted the best scratches (0.757) and a competitive inclusion (0.4896, essentially tied with the small-inclusion 0.493). My hypothesis was confirmed: the default 0.6-1.4 recipe extended the mAP50 lead rather than the small-inclusion variant. Candidate 2 (push inclusion to 40) was a clear loss — inclusion AP50 actually fell to 0.4101 and mAP50 dropped, so 30 is the sweet spot and starving patches/scratches to 5 hurt the whole. Candidate 3 (small patches) did recover the patches dip (0.8005, best patches) but at the cost of inclusion (0.4521) and scratches (0.6213), a net mAP50 loss. The default recipe is the strongest final model.",
  "choice": 1,
  "lesson": "Default-scale inclusion (0.6-1.4) at 30/10/10 is the best recipe: it extended mAP50 to 0.6614 with the best scratches (0.757) and competitive inclusion (0.490), beating the small-inclusion variant. Pushing inclusion to 40 (candidate 2) backfired — inclusion AP50 fell to 0.410 and mAP50 dropped, so 30 is the sweet spot and 5/5 starvation of the other classes is not worth it. Small patches (candidate 3) recovered patches (0.800) but cost inclusion and scratches, a net loss; the default recipe is the final bet."
}
